WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.300
The source and the drawing describe one authored diagram.

00:00:04.420 --> 00:00:10.870
A visual edit changes Plant UML text, and a source edit
changes the resulting picture.

00:00:10.990 --> 00:00:18.115
In this lesson we inspect labels and aliases, make changes
from both sides and use shared undo and redo.

00:00:18.235 --> 00:00:25.885
We will also break one line deliberately, read the
diagnostic and repair it while the last valid preview
remains available.

00:00:26.005 --> 00:00:31.505
Work in a disposable practice copy so each experiment is
easy to inspect.

00:00:35.033 --> 00:00:39.008
The start and end delimiters identify the diagram.

00:00:39.128 --> 00:00:45.828
Each task declaration gives the reader a display label and
can provide an alias for later references.

00:00:45.948 --> 00:00:53.998
In Design as design, the first label is what people read,
while the alias gives the source a stable identity to
reference.

00:00:54.118 --> 00:00:58.668
The dependency statements use those identities to connect
work.

00:00:58.788 --> 00:01:05.088
Keep the labels clear, preserve references deliberately
and check the current preview after editing.

00:01:08.600 --> 00:01:13.975
Select Design in the picture and change its duration from
five days to seven.

00:01:14.095 --> 00:01:20.695
Leave the field to apply the supported inspector edit,
then inspect the generated task declaration.

00:01:20.815 --> 00:01:26.615
The visual control updates the authored source; it does
not create a separate hidden schedule.

00:01:26.735 --> 00:01:33.310
Check following work as well as the selected bar, because
a dependency can carry the effect downstream.

00:01:33.430 --> 00:01:37.055
Keep unrelated labels and statements intact.

00:01:40.567 --> 00:01:44.267
Select Design and keep the original source visible.

00:01:44.387 --> 00:01:50.837
Type a temporary label while the name field retains focus;
the source still contains the committed name.

00:01:50.957 --> 00:01:56.732
Undo inside that text field and verify that Design returns
without a diagram change.

00:01:56.852 --> 00:02:03.727
Enter a negative duration and leave the field; an invalid
value must not replace the last valid schedule.

00:02:03.847 --> 00:02:08.722
Correct the duration to seven days and verify the
committed task statement.

00:02:08.842 --> 00:02:13.842
Now focus the source editor and use shared Undo to restore
five days.

00:02:13.962 --> 00:02:17.537
Redo returns the committed seven day edit.

00:02:17.657 --> 00:02:24.657
The focused text field, an invalid draft and the shared
diagram history are different states to check
deliberately.

00:02:28.167 --> 00:02:31.242
Now make the change directly in source.

00:02:31.362 --> 00:02:36.962
Replace Design’s five day duration with six days and wait
for Preview current.

00:02:37.082 --> 00:02:40.957
Select the same task and inspect its duration field.

00:02:41.077 --> 00:02:45.052
It should agree with the authored statement and the
current drawing.

00:02:45.172 --> 00:02:53.047
When those views disagree during a failed render, read the
status before assuming the visible picture represents the
newest source.

00:02:53.167 --> 00:02:58.467
Source ownership means that both editing routes must
preserve the same intended model.

00:03:01.967 --> 00:03:07.017
Rename the visible Design task to Interaction design in
its inspector.

00:03:07.137 --> 00:03:11.912
Inspect the task declaration and its dependencies after
the edit.

00:03:12.032 --> 00:03:18.357
The display label changes while the design alias remains
available to the connected statements.

00:03:18.477 --> 00:03:25.877
A label edit is different from a semantic alias rename,
which updates recognized references and has a preview.

00:03:25.997 --> 00:03:29.572
Later editor assistance shows that workflow.

00:03:29.692 --> 00:03:35.092
For now, verify the label, the stable alias and the
resulting schedule together.

00:03:38.600 --> 00:03:44.225
Place the caret on a task identifier in a scheduling rule
and open its symbol menu.

00:03:44.345 --> 00:03:49.945
Find references gathers the declaration and its uses
without changing the source.

00:03:50.065 --> 00:03:56.890
Select the declaration to jump to its source line and
inspect the scheduling rules that use the same alias.

00:03:57.010 --> 00:04:03.385
These actions help you inspect the consequences of an edit
before renaming or deleting a symbol.

00:04:06.900 --> 00:04:12.500
Change the duration visually, then return focus to the
source editor and use Undo.

00:04:12.620 --> 00:04:16.320
Verify that the duration returns to five days.

00:04:16.440 --> 00:04:19.990
Redo the edit and verify seven days again.

00:04:20.110 --> 00:04:26.010
This demonstrates one authored edit history across the
visual control and source editor.

00:04:26.130 --> 00:04:32.630
Be aware of focus: a field can have its own text-editing
behavior while you are still entering a value.

00:04:32.750 --> 00:04:40.425
Inspect the source after undoing a meaningful change,
rather than assuming the keyboard action affected the
intended edit.

00:04:43.933 --> 00:04:49.483
Replace the duration with the invalid word banana in this
disposable copy.

00:04:49.603 --> 00:04:53.678
Wait for the failed render and read Line eight cannot be
drawn.

00:04:53.798 --> 00:04:59.698
The message identifies the statement, while the drawing
retains the last version that worked.

00:04:59.818 --> 00:05:04.868
Switch to Diagram view, then use Go to line eight to visit
the reported source.

00:05:04.988 --> 00:05:09.738
A visible picture is not proof that the newest text
rendered successfully.

00:05:09.858 --> 00:05:15.358
Check the status, the reported line and the actual
statement before choosing a repair.

00:05:18.867 --> 00:05:23.817
Restore the numeric duration to five days and wait for
Preview current.

00:05:23.937 --> 00:05:29.887
Verify that the task statement is intact and that the
error no longer controls the preview.

00:05:30.007 --> 00:05:35.357
A repair should preserve the model you intended, not
merely make the error count smaller.

00:05:35.477 --> 00:05:41.777
The editor assistance lesson adds error navigation,
explanation and proposed quick fixes.

00:05:41.897 --> 00:05:48.297
The habit is the same: understand the statement, make a
deliberate edit and check the current result.

00:05:51.800 --> 00:05:57.800
Pause and change the same duration visually and in source,
checking that the other view agrees.

00:05:57.920 --> 00:06:02.770
Rename a display label while preserving its alias and
dependencies.

00:06:02.890 --> 00:06:07.365
Undo and redo a visual edit with focus in the source
editor.

00:06:07.485 --> 00:06:13.910
Finally introduce one invalid duration, read the failed
line, use Go to line and repair it.

00:06:14.030 --> 00:06:22.455
You have succeeded when the source, inspector and current
preview agree, and you can explain why a retained picture
may be older than the current text.

00:06:25.967 --> 00:06:32.392
The source holds the authored model, while the picture and
inspectors make it easier to understand and edit.

00:06:32.512 --> 00:06:40.337
Use both editing routes deliberately, preserve identity
and references, and verify shared undo or redo in the
source.

00:06:40.457 --> 00:06:43.982
Read render status whenever a change fails.

00:06:44.102 --> 00:06:52.677
The next lessons apply this routine to tasks,
dependencies, people and forecasts so that the schedule
remains understandable as it grows.
